<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mukalla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mukalla is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the main seaport of the Hadramawt. In the 18th and 19th centuries it was ruled by the Kasadi family and then by the Qu'aiti sultans, who made it their capital under British protection. It is now the capital of Hadramawt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mukalla.jpg" alt="Mukalla" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammed Omar Al-Aidaroos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khor_al-Mukalla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mukalla lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>, on the shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, about 480 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, and is the most important port of the Hadhramaut region. It is not far from Qana, the ancient principal Hadrami trading post between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>-producing areas in its hinterland. The name comes from the Arabic verb kala, to preserve or nurture, reflecting its role as a sheltered harbour. Earlier names include Al-Kheesa, meaning bay or cove, Bandar Yaqoub, after the saint Yaqoub bin Yusuf, who settled there about the 12th century, and Bandar Al-Naqeeb, after Captain Salah bin Hamad Al-Kassadi of the dynasty that governed the town in the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mukalla was founded in 1035 as a fishing settlement. In the 19th and 20th centuries the Kathiri and Qu'aiti sultanates struggled for control of it, and it became the capital of the Qu'aiti State of Hadhramaut. The Qu'aiti Sultanate belonged to the Eastern Aden Protectorate, and a British Resident Advisor was stationed in the town. The sultanates' other major cities were Ash-Shihr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a>.</p><p>Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haine">Haines</a>, a British officer who surveyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in the 1830s, described Mukalla as a town of 4,500 inhabitants with a significant trade in slaves. Theodore Bent and Mabel Bent used it several times in the 1890s to enter and leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hadhramaut">Wadi Hadhramaut</a>, and Theodore Bent called it the only spot between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and Maskat with any claim to the name of port.</p><p>In 1967 Mukalla lost its status as capital of the Qu'aiti Sultanate when it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</a>. After Yemeni unification in 1990 it became part of the present state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mukalla is the capital city district of Hadhramaut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s largest governorate. The port lies east of the town, with an oil pipeline for tankers and a cement factory of an Omani-Yemeni company, and the main market souq is a commercial hub. The old town has the sultan's royal palace and guard towers that once served as outposts, and the Hadhramaut Mountains, such as Husn Ghuraf, lie nearby. The College of Medicine of Hadhramout University is in the city, and Riyan International Airport serves it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mukalla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century they established themselves as naqibs, or governors, of the port of <b>Mukalla</b> on the coast of Hadramawt and ruled it as a small independent state.</blockquote><a href="/state/kasadi-sultanate">Kasadi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>With Indian wealth they bought land and influence in Hadramawt, taking Shibam and the port of Shihr from around 1858, then <b>Mukalla</b> from the Kasadi Sultanate in 1881.</blockquote><a href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
